{
	title: 'Shared host-authored tool presentation for both clients',
	spec: 'Plugins can supply an optional host-authored tool presentation shared by terminal and web: a concise title and ordered text, code and diff blocks with optional file paths. Use the existing owned hook mechanism, not browser plugin JavaScript or a component framework. Presentation is display-only, recorded with calls/results and sent through existing live/snapshot paths; provider input remains unchanged. Historical records without presentation use existing rendering. Text references into existing results avoid duplicating large output. Generate presentation when the call/result changes, not during redraw or history scrolling. Initial titles describe intent; completed views reflect verified outcomes, with execution status and elapsed time independent of custom content. Both UIs show readable file-oriented changes rather than argument scaffolding, retain full raw call/result access and complete errors, preserve copy/select and keyboard/touch expansion, and handle narrow/long views. Presentation failures report complete errors and fall back to ordinary tool rendering without rerunning or misreporting execution. Validate presentation data at its boundary and persisted records. Only opt-in tools change; other visualizers remain untouched. Exercise the extension with a generic multi-file editing fixture and shared rendering tests; no dependencies. Keep native renderer hooks available for future specialized interfaces.',
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		'Host plugins wrap toolPresentations.present(call, result?) in src/host/tool-presentation.ts. Return undefined for the existing view, or {title, blocks:[{kind: text|code|diff, path?, language?, text}]} where text is literal or a UTF-16 output slice {from,to}. actions.arrived attaches intent to calls and tools.run attaches completion after output capping. Optional presentation/presentationError survive history and transcript folding; replay strips presentation before provider conversion. Failures retain execution outcome, full stack, input and invalid presentation, and reach both user and model. The terminal and web retain native layout; raw details use the existing inline fold phase for opted-in tools, plus a web button. Closed web cards defer block rendering. No migration or browser plugin loader; old records remain ordinary views.',
